CAKE (Common Applications Kept Enhanced) is a queue scheduler that
combines flow and host fairness, diffserv priority tins, the COBALT
AQM and link-layer overhead compensation. This plugin lets pipes of
the traffic shaper use it in place of FQ-CoDel.

The scheduler is an unofficial kernel module (cake-kmod) built for a
specific OPNsense series. If it does not match the running kernel,
the shaper keeps running with the pipes' own scheduler. From 1.4.1, the
plugin uses dummynet-st by default instead: dummynet with CAKE built in,
which releases packets between clock ticks; when that does not match the
kernel, the stock dummynet and cake-kmod are used.

Plugin Changelog
================

1.4.6

* When cake-kmod or dummynet-st does not match the running kernel, the
  CAKE page names both kernels (this one, and the one the package is
  built for) and says the fallback lasts until the package is updated.
  cake-kmod 1.5.6 and dummynet-st 1.0.6 record the kernel they are built
  for in full (kernel.version), taken from OPNsense's kernel set rather
  than the build machine

1.4.5

* Crash-loop guard: while dummynet-st is in use, a marker in /conf says
  so, and a clean shutdown removes it. A boot that finds it left behind
  after a panic (from the kernel's message buffer), or after two boots in
  a row that ended without a clean shutdown (power cuts, resets, hangs),
  uses the stock dummynet, and the CAKE page says why, until Apply there.
  A plain reboot from a shell counts as clean
* The shaper wrapper runs one at a time: boot, configd reloads and Apply
  starting it at once no longer interleave unloads and loads (a run waits
  up to two minutes for the one before)
* "Sub-tick release" is labelled beta on the page, with how to turn it
  off; the README says the wrapper swaps a forked dummynet in at runtime

1.4.4

* dummynet-st 1.0.4, from a second review: unloading dummynet_st.ko can
  no longer leave dummynet's tick timer armed into freed memory (a race
  inherited from the stock dummynet, which the fork meets more often as
  it is swapped on shaper reloads); bursts near the largest accepted
  are bounded so that reconfiguring a busy pipe cannot overflow its
  credit

1.4.3

* dummynet-st 1.0.3, from a review of its release timer: unloading
  dummynet_st.ko (switching to the stock dummynet, or removing the
  package) now waits for packets still being processed before it stops
  the timer, which could otherwise be re-armed or used after it was
  freed; a pipe left unserved for over half an hour (a suspended VM)
  no longer overflows its credit and stalls; very large bursts and
  delays are bounded before the fork's finer units overflow them

1.4.2

* Sub-tick release for all pipes: a new choice on the CAKE page,
  "Sub-tick release for", loads dummynet-st whenever the shaper has
  pipes, not only while a CAKE entry is enabled, so that a shaper with
  FQ-CoDel or another scheduler only releases between ticks too, and its
  CoDel or PIE measures delay with the precise clock. The default stays
  "Pipes with CAKE"

1.4.1

(1.4.0 was not released.)

* Sub-tick release, on by default: with dummynet-st (a new dependency),
  the shaper loads dummynet_st.ko in place of the stock dummynet.ko and
  dn_cake.ko, which releases packets every 250 us (the release interval
  on the CAKE page) rather than once per clock tick, so packets wait
  less and leave in smaller bursts, on every pipe. Switch it off on the
  CAKE page to keep the stock dummynet
* The stock dummynet and dn_cake.ko are used when dummynet-st does not
  match the kernel or does not load, or after a crash while it was
  loading (Apply on the CAKE page tries it again), with the reason on
  the CAKE page and in the system log
* cake-kmod 1.5.1: CAKE's link clock keeps the link's time through a late
  release, so tin shares hold when the router's CPU is busy

1.3.4

* Upgrades take effect without disabling and re-enabling the CAKE
  entries: on a shaper reload, a loaded dn_cake.ko that is not the
  installed one is unloaded and the new one loaded. cake-kmod 1.4.5
  reloads the shaper when it is installed or upgraded
* The CAKE page warns when the loaded dn_cake.ko is not the installed one
* A "CAKE shaper" entry in the services list and the dashboard's Services
  widget, while a CAKE entry is enabled: running when every enabled
  entry's pipe uses CAKE, stopped when any is left on its own scheduler
  (see the CAKE page for why); its restart button reloads the shaper

1.3.3

* A pipe with a mask is left on its own scheduler, with the reason in
  the system log and on the CAKE page: a mask makes a CAKE instance,
  with its own flow tables, per host
* The memory limit is at most 256 MB (4096 before); a larger configured
  value is lowered to 256 MB, with a warning on the CAKE page

1.3.2

* The statistics tables keep fixed column widths, so the columns line
  up between pipes and do not move as the values change

1.3.1

* The statistics no longer disappear and reset between bursts of
  traffic. dummynet destroys a scheduler instance as soon as nothing is
  queued; cake-kmod 1.3.1 keeps the instance's tables, AQM state and
  counters with the pipe for the next one, instead of reallocating them
  for nearly every packet on a quiet link
* A pipe rate changed without reconfiguring its scheduler now reaches
  CAKE's tin rates and AQM parameters
* The CAKE page lists pipes that have not carried traffic yet

1.3

* Per-tin statistics on the CAKE page, as tc -s shows them: rate
  threshold, peak, average and base delay, packets, bytes, backlog,
  drops, ECN marks, ACK filter drops, flow counts and hash collisions
  (needs cake-kmod 1.3)
* Only load CAKE if the dummynet.ko in use is the one cake-kmod was
  built against (recorded from cake-kmod 1.3 on), so an update that
  changes dummynet within a FreeBSD release cannot meet a mismatched
  module
* cake-kmod 1.3: COBALT and the flow lifecycle as in Linux sch_cake,
  checked bit for bit against it; ECN marking only changes a writable
  header within the first mbuf

1.2

* The CAKE page warns when kern.hz is below 1000, as it is by default
  in a virtual machine, and says how to raise it

1.1

* Each scheduler instance runs on its own copy of the configuration,
  so a failed table rebuild no longer mixes old tables with new
  parameters, and a rate change reaches a queued backlog at once
* Failed table allocations are logged at most once a second

1.0

* Initial release
